WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.820
From animated favorites to holiday classics,&nbsp;
these movies are beloved nowadays — but you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.940
wouldn't be able to tell if you just saw&nbsp;
how much money they made in theaters.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.000
The bizarrely charming "Willy&nbsp;
Wonka &amp; the Chocolate Factory"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.560
wasn't a hit when it came out in 1971.&nbsp;
Although it's considered a classic now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.900
it only earned about $4 million upon its release&nbsp;
— which wasn't great, considering its $3 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.620
budget. This may have happened because it&nbsp;
was marketed as a family-friendly film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:29.135
even though it had no shortage of moments&nbsp;
that could be considered scary for children.

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.545
"Don't just stand there, do something!"

00:00:32.239 --> 00:00:34.459
"Help. Police. Murder."

00:00:34.459 --> 00:00:39.300
Roald Dahl, the author of the 1964 book the&nbsp;
film was based on, was outspoken about his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.860
dislike of the film adaptation. He didn't like&nbsp;
the songs, the numerous changes to the plot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:47.580
or how the focus seemed to be on Willy&nbsp;
Wonka rather than Charlie Bucket.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:52.800
"Willy Wonka &amp; the Chocolate Factory" became much&nbsp;
more popular after its re-release in 1996 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.840
availability on home video. It's now considered&nbsp;
one of Gene Wilder's most iconic roles — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.400
that's saying a lot, considering he has no&nbsp;
shortage of unforgettable performances. The film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.440
popularity led to the 2005 remake "Charlie and the&nbsp;
Chocolate Factory" starring Johnny Depp and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.120
prequel "Wonka" starring Timothée Chalamet,&nbsp;
which is set for release in December 2023.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:16.560
It may be the movie with the longest-ever&nbsp;
theatrical run, but "The Rocky Horror Picture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.300
Show" most definitely bombed during its initial&nbsp;
release. Even though it was based on a popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:28.080
stage musical, the film adaptation only earned&nbsp;
$21,245 in its opening weekend. Disappointed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.755
the premiere, 20th Century Fox pulled the movie&nbsp;
from several other locations around the country.

00:01:32.755 --> 00:01:36.960
For most films, that would have been the end of&nbsp;
the story. But one advertising executive had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:41.280
brilliant idea: start showing "The Rocky Horror&nbsp;
Picture Show" at one of the popular "midnight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.185
shows" at the Waverly Theatre in New York&nbsp;
City. When they did, it proved an instant hit.

00:01:46.185 --> 00:01:50.700
After several months of late-night showings with&nbsp;
a party-like atmosphere, one of Waverly's "Rocky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.440
Horror" regulars — mild-mannered kindergarten&nbsp;
teacher Louis Farese — started to shout improvised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.820
lines back at the screen. The midnight showings&nbsp;
soon spread to other theaters around the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:04.260
along with the habit of shouted audience&nbsp;
"call-backs." Eventually, dedicated fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.940
began to form "shadow-cast" performance troupes&nbsp;
— acting out the film in costume on stage as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:13.020
movie plays on the big screen behind them.&nbsp;
Within a year, "Rocky Horror" went from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:17.824
box-office bomb to a cult sensation that's&nbsp;
still going strong more than 40 years later.

00:02:17.824 --> 00:02:22.200
It's hard to believe that "The Wizard of Oz,"&nbsp;
one of the most beloved and influential films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:27.300
of all time, bombed at the box office — but&nbsp;
it's true. When the 1939 classic was released,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:33.240
it made a mere $3 million against a production&nbsp;
and distribution budget of over $4 million. People&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:37.500
just weren't rushing to the theaters to see a&nbsp;
farm girl explore an alternate world with a lion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:42.480
a scarecrow, and a tin man. On the bright side,&nbsp;
it mostly earned positive reviews and went on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.456
win two Academy Awards: one for best original&nbsp;
song and the other for best original score.

00:02:47.456 --> 00:02:53.700
"You ungrateful creatures! Think yourselves&nbsp;
lucky that I'm giving you audience tomorrow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:56.693
instead of twenty years from now!"

00:02:56.693 --> 00:03:01.500
The film started making profits after it was&nbsp;
re-released in 1949, thanks in part to Judy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:07.020
Garland's elevated stardom. It earned even more&nbsp;
money after it was re-released again in 1955,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:12.360
aired on TV beginning in 1956, and came&nbsp;
out on home video in 1980. Nowadays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:17.400
it's an undeniable classic and has made&nbsp;
around $25.6 million overall — not bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.200
considering its original budget. The film has also&nbsp;
inspired multiple remakes and spin-offs, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:27.000
the 2013 film "Oz the Great and Powerful" and&nbsp;
the long-running Broadway musical "Wicked."

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.740
Many of us grew up watching "Hocus Pocus" every&nbsp;
Halloween, so it might come as a surprise that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:37.860
the cult classic wasn't an instant hit upon its&nbsp;
1993 release. The film grossed about $45 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:42.060
so it managed to recoup its $28 million&nbsp;
budget — but not by much when compared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:45.780
to Disney's other holiday films. For&nbsp;
instance, Disney had released "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:50.940
Santa Clause" the following year, which made&nbsp;
almost $200 million on a $22 million budget.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:54.480
"Hocus Pocus" should've been released&nbsp;
around Halloween, but it was released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:59.280
in July instead — a decision that essentially set&nbsp;
it up to fail. Disney had thought a summer release&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:03.180
would be better for the movie, as kids would&nbsp;
be out of school. From a practical standpoint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:06.720
a July release would mean that it wouldn't&nbsp;
compete with their other spooky film set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.800
to come out that year: "The Nightmare Before&nbsp;
Christmas." While Tim Burton's holiday mashup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:15.240
had nearly quadrupled its budget by the time it&nbsp;
left theaters, "Hocus Pocus" wasn't so lucky.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:18.960
Despite its underwhelming premiere,&nbsp;
"Hocus Pocus" eventually put a spell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.800
on viewers. It started airing on TV&nbsp;
every Halloween in the late '90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.960
and it gained even more fans after its&nbsp;
2002 DVD release. The sequel "Hocus Pocus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:33.180
2" came out in 2022, starring the original&nbsp;
trio of actors as the Sanderson sisters.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:38.220
Ridley Scott's sci-fi epic "Blade Runner" left a&nbsp;
huge impact on cinema and is considered nowadays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:41.940
to be a science fiction masterpiece.&nbsp;
Despite its current reputation, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:47.760
it only grossed about $26 million in the&nbsp;
summer of 1982 on a $28 million budget. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:51.880
make matters worse, many people who saw the&nbsp;
movie didn't have great things to say about&nbsp;it. 

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:56.136
Some viewers thought the pacing was slow&nbsp;
and others didn't like how grim the story was.

00:04:56.136 --> 00:05:01.956
"I've had people walk out on me before,&nbsp;
but not when I was being so charming."

00:05:01.956 --> 00:05:05.400
Many commercially successful films&nbsp;
have simple plots and tons of action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:09.360
but Scott was more interested in making an&nbsp;
artistic film with thought-provoking themes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:13.860
"Blade Runner" also had stiff competition when&nbsp;
it was released: "E.T. the Extra-Terrestrial,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:18.734
"Star Trek II: Wrath of Khan," and "Conan the&nbsp;
Barbarian" all came out around the same time.

00:05:18.734 --> 00:05:22.620
As time went on, "Blade Runner" gained a&nbsp;
sizable following and was recut multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:27.660
times, ending up with about $39.5&nbsp;
million. Its popularity inspired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:32.580
the 2017 sequel "Blade Runner: 2049,"&nbsp;
the 2021 animated series "Blade Runner:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:36.240
Black Lotus," and the upcoming&nbsp;
live-action series "Blade Runner 2099."

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:40.620
"Children of Men" has powerful themes about&nbsp;
the resilience of the human spirit that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:45.180
just as relevant now as they were when&nbsp;
the film came out in 2006. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:48.840
Alfonso Cuarón's masterpiece wasn't able&nbsp;
to recoup the cost of its production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:53.220
earning around $70.5 million&nbsp;
on a $76 million budget.

00:05:53.479 --> 00:05:56.599
One reason for this was the&nbsp;
film's limited theatrical release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.599 --> 00:06:00.348
which meant many people couldn't see it&nbsp;
even if they wanted to. On top of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.348 --> 00:06:03.840
Universal Pictures didn't market the&nbsp;
movie successfully. They weren't sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.820
what demographic wanted to see a bleak dystopian&nbsp;
sci-fi movie devoid of flashy laser guns and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:12.180
flying spaceships, and their promotional&nbsp;
campaign reflected their uncertainty.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:15.660
On the plus side, "Children of Men"&nbsp;
has since gotten the recognition it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:18.660
deserves. The film was met with&nbsp;
glowing reviews upon its release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:24.300
and it has an impressive critic score of 92% on&nbsp;
Rotten Tomatoes. It won multiple awards as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:28.680
including best science fiction film at the Saturn&nbsp;
Awards and best cinematography at the BAFTAs.

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:33.360
"Fear and Loathing in Las Vegas" is one of&nbsp;
those movies that people either love or hate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:37.620
Unfortunately for director Terry Gilliam and&nbsp;
the hundreds of people who worked on the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:43.620
most people hated it when it came out in 1998.&nbsp;
It earned a mere $13.7 million in theaters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:48.240
failing to break even on its $18.5&nbsp;
million budget. The movie's style of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:52.560
storytelling isn't for everyone; it's chaotic,&nbsp;
trippy, and downright confusing at times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:56.520
meant to mimic the altered state of mind that&nbsp;
the characters are in due to their constant drug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:01.200
usage. Regardless of the intent, some audiences&nbsp;
find it more overwhelming than entertaining.

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:04.680
"Fear and Loathing in Las Vegas" gained&nbsp;
a cult following after it was released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:09.600
on DVD. Johnny Depp, who starred as Raoul&nbsp;
Duke, had since skyrocketed in popularity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:13.920
after portraying Captain Jack Sparrow in&nbsp;
2003's "Pirates of the Caribbean" — and as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:17.400
the Black Pearl sailed into movie history,&nbsp;
"Fear and Loathing" followed in its wake.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:21.240
Additionally, movies using the altered&nbsp;
stream-of-consciousness style had become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:25.320
more mainstream in the past years, so it&nbsp;
wasn't as jarring for viewers anymore. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:29.280
allowed audiences to admire the film for its&nbsp;
unique style and themes of self-indulgence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:32.520
disillusionment, and the constant&nbsp;
search for the American dream.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:35.160
"I mean, if a thing's worth doing, it's worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:39.204
doing right. This is the&nbsp;
American dream in action."

00:07:39.204 --> 00:07:44.460
When Paul Verhoeven's "Starship Troopers" came out&nbsp;
in 1997, its marketing made it seem as though it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:49.080
would be a goofy science-fiction adventure rather&nbsp;
than a satire of the military-industrial complex.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:53.100
Thanks in part to this bait-and-switch, poor&nbsp;
word of mouth led to the movie earning a mere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:59.220
$54.8 million during its domestic release —&nbsp;
slightly over half of its $105 million budget.

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:04.140
"Starship Troopers" didn't have a star-studded&nbsp;
cast to draw audiences in: the lead actors were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:08.640
Casper Van Dien and Denise Richards, who weren't&nbsp;
big names at the time. The film was also quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:13.500
different from the 1959 novel by Robert Heinlein&nbsp;
that it was based on, which definitely threw some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:18.360
people off. The book depicted military life in a&nbsp;
positive light, and audiences may have assumed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:22.800
film would do the same — nothing could be further&nbsp;
from the truth. What really put the nail in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:26.580
coffin, though, was the movie's release date:&nbsp;
it came out the same year as "The Lost World:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:31.176
Jurassic Park" and a month before "Titanic,"&nbsp;
so it had a tough time gaining notoriety.

00:08:31.176 --> 00:08:34.380
Although "Starship Troopers" was&nbsp;
vastly misunderstood upon its release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:38.340
it eventually gained popularity among&nbsp;
a niche audience. It eventually went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:43.980
on to reach a final gross of $121 million&nbsp;
overall thanks to international screenings.

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:48.420
The animated family film "The Iron Giant" has&nbsp;
gained an impressive audience since its release&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:54.000
in 1999, but it was initially a huge commercial&nbsp;
failure. Brad Bird's beloved tearjerker only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:59.400
made about $31.3 million against a $50 million&nbsp;
budget due to poor marketing from Warner Bros.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:03.600
They had recently released the film "Quest for&nbsp;
Camelot" which had bombed at the box office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:07.980
so they weren't willing to spend a lot of money&nbsp;
promoting another animated movie. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:11.460
hardly anyone seemed to notice when&nbsp;
"The Iron Giant" was finally released.

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:15.720
Despite all of that, the movie was adored&nbsp;
by those who did go to see it. Fans and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:19.140
critics alike praised the film for its&nbsp;
beautiful animation, likable characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:24.180
and balance of funny and heart-wrenching moments.&nbsp;
By the time it was released on DVD, it had already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:28.800
established a following — but being available on&nbsp;
home video helped it reach an even wider audience.

00:09:29.037 --> 00:09:32.457
"You're a good guy. Like Superman."

00:09:32.939 --> 00:09:34.280
"Superman."

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:38.760
"Treasure Planet" was packed with&nbsp;
adventure, quirky characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:42.600
and beautifully animated scenes, so it&nbsp;
should've been a box office hit — but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:47.160
that wasn't the case. The film cost&nbsp;
a whopping $140 million to make and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:52.140
grossed about $110 million, costing Disney&nbsp;
a pretty penny after its 2002 release.

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:57.420
Unfortunately, this movie was doomed to fail: it&nbsp;
was set for release around the same time as "Harry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:02.280
Potter and the Chamber of Secrets," "The Santa&nbsp;
Clause 2," and "Die Another Day." Disney also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:07.140
didn't market the film very well, and its target&nbsp;
audience was unclear. Despite being promoted as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:11.640
family film, the plot is a bit too complicated for&nbsp;
children, the main character is a moody teenager,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:15.540
and there are no princesses or musical&nbsp;
numbers to be found. At the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:20.040
its perceived status as a kids' movie kept the&nbsp;
older crowd from flocking to theaters to see it.

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:24.360
Although "Treasure Planet" garnered mixed reviews&nbsp;
and tanked at the box office, people started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:29.580
recognizing its unique charm after it came out on&nbsp;
DVD. They came to love the film's mature themes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:34.680
relatable characters, and stunning animation&nbsp;
style that mixes hand-drawn frames with CGI.

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:38.520
Countless films have faded into obscurity&nbsp;
over the years, but "Citizen Kane" has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:42.240
remained highly renowned for decades.&nbsp;
It wasn't always so popular, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:46.560
as it earned about $1.6 million&nbsp;
against its $800,000 budget when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:51.960
it came out in 1941. That made Orson Welles'&nbsp;
masterpiece not much of a financial success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:57.002
despite the many positive reviews it received for&nbsp;
its outstanding performances and cinematography.

00:10:57.002 --> 00:10:58.862
"I did lose a million dollars last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.862 --> 00:11:02.556
I expect to lose a million dollars this year.&nbsp;
I expect to lose a million dollars next year!"

00:11:02.556 --> 00:11:06.600
The main reason for the disappointing turnout&nbsp;
was the controversy surrounding the film prior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:10.920
to its release. Powerful publishing tycoon&nbsp;
William Randolph Hearst was offended by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:14.880
his similarity to the movie's troubled&nbsp;
main character, Charles Foster Kane — so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:18.780
he did everything he could to stop the&nbsp;
film from being released. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:22.500
it had a delayed and limited rollout,&nbsp;
which severely impacted its box office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:26.940
performance. It did, however, win best&nbsp;
original screenplay at the Academy Awards.

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:31.320
"Citizen Kane" gained a lot more praise&nbsp;
after it was re-released in the 1950s and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:36.240
began airing on TV. As the years went on, it&nbsp;
became considered one of the best of the best,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:40.466
influencing countless other films and&nbsp;
earning its place in cinematic history.

00:11:40.466 --> 00:11:43.620
When it comes to Christmas movies,&nbsp;
"It's a Wonderful Life" is a go-to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:47.580
for many families year after year.&nbsp;
When the movie came out in 1946,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:51.300
though, it was such a failure that it led&nbsp;
to director Frank Capra's production company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:56.220
shutting down. The future holiday classic&nbsp;
originally had a budget of about $2 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.220 --> 00:12:00.300
but multiple script rewrites and changes to&nbsp;
the crew nearly doubled the cost of production.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:06.000
It earned a measly $3.3 million upon its&nbsp;
1946 release and garnered mixed reviews.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:10.200
"It's a Wonderful Life" was released a&nbsp;
few years after World War II had ended,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:15.360
which contributed to its unimpressive box office&nbsp;
numbers. American audiences were not necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:19.980
in the mood for a supposedly inspirational&nbsp;
holiday movie that eventually has a happy ending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:24.840
but also features more than a few shockingly&nbsp;
dark moments. The film also came out around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:28.260
the same time as "The Best Years of Our&nbsp;
Lives," which blew it out of the water.

00:12:28.260 --> 00:12:33.840
"It's a Wonderful Life" didn't become popular&nbsp;
until decades later. Its copyright ended in 1974,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:37.920
so TV channels started airing it every Christmas&nbsp;
because they didn't have to pay any royalties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:43.096
for it. People began realizing how good of&nbsp;
a film it truly is, and the rest is history.

